1
00:00:02,611 --> 00:00:04,221
[indistinct conversations,
police radio chatter]

2
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:10,097
[Cho]
The attack took place
in the middle of a shift change.

3
00:00:10,184 --> 00:00:11,620
Normally the shift
works till morning,

4
00:00:11,707 --> 00:00:14,188
but some of the crew
had a bachelor party tonight.

5
00:00:15,624 --> 00:00:17,539
Who's getting married?

6
00:00:17,626 --> 00:00:19,280
Toby Rawlins.

7
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:21,847
He found the victim seconds
after his throat was cut.

8
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:24,633
Mm. That's gotta be
the worst bachelor party ever.

9
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,156
[Teresa]
'Did toby see what happened?'

10
00:00:26,243 --> 00:00:28,463
He saw a hooded man
run into the woods.

11
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:30,726
Jeans, dark jacket.
Couldn't make out a face.

12
00:00:30,813 --> 00:00:32,423
Any sign of a murder weapon?

13
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:34,164
Nothing.

14
00:00:34,251 --> 00:00:37,559
- Who's the victim?
- Paul Satterfield.

15
00:00:37,646 --> 00:00:40,649
25 years on the job,
multiple citations for bravery.

16
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:50,702
It's not your fault.

17
00:00:50,789 --> 00:00:52,965
Yeah, I know that.

18
00:00:53,053 --> 00:00:55,316
Unless, of course,
you did it.

19
00:00:55,403 --> 00:00:57,622
- Did you kill him?
- No!

20
00:00:57,709 --> 00:00:59,015
Okay.
Just had to ask.

21
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:02,845
What are you doing
out here, anyway?

22
00:01:02,932 --> 00:01:05,630
Don't you have to celebrate
your last day of freedom?

23
00:01:05,717 --> 00:01:07,502
Paul couldn't make the party.

24
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:09,417
He never leaves
his shift early.

25
00:01:09,504 --> 00:01:12,942
But he bought me a cigar
for us to smoke together.

26
00:01:13,029 --> 00:01:15,988
So someone knew the victim
was coming out here?

27
00:01:16,076 --> 00:01:19,209
If you're saying one of
our guys did this, no way.

28
00:01:19,296 --> 00:01:21,559
I saw the killer
get away through there.

29
00:01:21,646 --> 00:01:24,084
Look, we had half the fire
station looking for him.

30
00:01:24,171 --> 00:01:25,694
Guys from two shifts.

31
00:01:25,781 --> 00:01:27,870
Could be the killer
circled back around

32
00:01:27,957 --> 00:01:30,090
and joined the search
for himself.

33
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:32,918
Could have happened. It would
have been chaotic out here.

34
00:01:33,005 --> 00:01:35,704
Paul Satterfield
was one of us.

35
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:37,488
We all loved him
like a brother.

36
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:41,013
Yeah. You've heard of
Cain and Abel, right?

37
00:01:41,101 --> 00:01:43,799
Cho, gather the firefighters.
We need to establish a timeline.

38
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:45,105
Can I borrow
your flashlight?

39
00:01:45,192 --> 00:01:47,107
I'm gonna go looking
for the murder weapon.

40
00:01:50,762 --> 00:01:53,591
[crickets chirping]

41
00:01:56,899 --> 00:01:59,771
[howling in distance]

42
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:22,229
Aah!

43
00:02:28,583 --> 00:02:30,541
[grunting]

44
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:41,204
[choking]

45
00:02:49,125 --> 00:02:50,605
Jane?

46
00:02:51,867 --> 00:02:54,522
Jane, you out here?

47
00:02:54,609 --> 00:02:57,002
Jane, come on!

48
00:03:03,792 --> 00:03:05,141
No.

49
00:03:12,192 --> 00:03:13,497
Paramedics!

50
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:15,934
I need help now!
Please!

51
00:03:20,374 --> 00:03:22,158
[theme music]

52
00:03:30,993 --> 00:03:32,212
[male #1]
Still not breathing.
No pulse.

53
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:33,517
- Is he prone to seizures?
- No.

54
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:41,351
How long has he
been out here?

55
00:03:41,438 --> 00:03:45,137
I don't know.
Um, seven, eight minutes, maybe.

56
00:03:45,225 --> 00:03:47,618
- Defibrillator, standing by.
- Clear.

57
00:03:51,361 --> 00:03:52,841
Please.

58
00:03:52,928 --> 00:03:55,409
[indistinct chattering]

59
00:04:02,067 --> 00:04:03,025
Everybody clear.

60
00:04:04,635 --> 00:04:05,680
[pops loudly]

61
00:04:09,379 --> 00:04:10,641
Still nothing.

62
00:04:10,728 --> 00:04:11,773
Clear.

63
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:12,904
[pops loudly]

64
00:04:14,602 --> 00:04:17,082
[gags]

65
00:04:17,169 --> 00:04:19,520
- Jane... Jane, are you okay?
- Got a rhythm.

66
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:23,088
Bring that O2 up.

67
00:04:23,175 --> 00:04:25,047
Can you hear me? Jane?

68
00:04:29,094 --> 00:04:30,661
Get him ready
for transport.

69
00:04:30,748 --> 00:04:34,926
On my count.
One, two, three, lift.

70
00:04:35,013 --> 00:04:36,667
- Coming through.
- Hey, watch yourselves.

71
00:04:36,754 --> 00:04:40,454
Make a hole, make a hole.
Coming through.

72
00:04:40,541 --> 00:04:41,585
How is he?

73
00:04:41,672 --> 00:04:43,065
He's breathing.

74
00:04:43,152 --> 00:04:45,676
He's gonna be fine.
He'll be fine.

75
00:04:45,763 --> 00:04:48,592
[monitor beeping steadily]

76
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:57,297
Jane?

77
00:05:03,172 --> 00:05:04,434
How you feeling?

78
00:05:05,827 --> 00:05:07,481
Excellent, I think.

79
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:11,789
It's good to see
you breathing.

80
00:05:11,876 --> 00:05:14,923
It's good to
see you, period.

81
00:05:15,010 --> 00:05:18,405
We're doing everything we can
to find your attacker.

82
00:05:18,492 --> 00:05:20,711
You didn't happen
to see a face, did you?

83
00:05:20,798 --> 00:05:23,410
No.

84
00:05:23,497 --> 00:05:25,281
Not that I can remember.

85
00:05:25,368 --> 00:05:27,065
What's the last thing
you do remember?

86
00:05:35,117 --> 00:05:36,727
Are we sleeping together?

87
00:05:36,814 --> 00:05:39,208
Excuse me?

88
00:05:39,295 --> 00:05:40,731
Well, you're a cop,
that's obvious

89
00:05:40,818 --> 00:05:42,777
but you're not treating me
like a suspect.

90
00:05:42,864 --> 00:05:45,562
And I can't see any other
reason for a police officer

91
00:05:45,649 --> 00:05:48,348
to come to my bedside
unless we're--

92
00:05:48,435 --> 00:05:49,784
Unless we're
sleeping together.

93
00:05:51,525 --> 00:05:53,614
You don't know who I am?

94
00:05:53,701 --> 00:05:56,443
Please don't take it personally.
I'm sure you're quite memorable.

95
00:05:56,530 --> 00:05:58,619
I just, I've been
through a lot..

96
00:05:58,706 --> 00:06:00,316
...apparently.

97
00:06:00,403 --> 00:06:02,579
No.

98
00:06:02,666 --> 00:06:05,234
We are not
sleeping together.

99
00:06:05,321 --> 00:06:07,149
We're working toward it,
though, right?

100
00:06:07,236 --> 00:06:08,411
So I haven't
missed anything?

101
00:06:10,108 --> 00:06:11,327
What's your name?

102
00:06:14,025 --> 00:06:15,287
Are you putting me on?

103
00:06:16,811 --> 00:06:17,855
I wish I was.

104
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:23,078
Um, I'm Teresa Lisbon
with the CBI.

105
00:06:23,165 --> 00:06:25,863
I'm a homicide detective.

106
00:06:25,950 --> 00:06:27,256
You're my consultant.

107
00:06:27,343 --> 00:06:29,650
I catch bad guys?
Wow, that sounds like fun.

108
00:06:29,737 --> 00:06:31,695
I always wanted to pit
my psychic skills

109
00:06:31,782 --> 00:06:32,783
against criminals.

110
00:06:32,870 --> 00:06:34,785
You're not a psychic.

111
00:06:34,872 --> 00:06:36,787
You used to pretend
to be one, but--

112
00:06:36,874 --> 00:06:38,093
But what?

113
00:06:39,790 --> 00:06:41,531
Teresa?

114
00:06:41,618 --> 00:06:43,881
Uh, I'm sorry.

115
00:06:43,968 --> 00:06:46,928
I should have talked to the
doctor before I came here. I--

116
00:06:47,015 --> 00:06:49,539
Whoa, whoa.
Teresa, wait.

117
00:06:49,626 --> 00:06:51,802
I saw something
during my attack.

118
00:06:51,889 --> 00:06:53,413
What did you see?

119
00:06:54,283 --> 00:06:56,285
A light.

120
00:06:56,372 --> 00:06:57,417
What kind of light?

121
00:06:59,157 --> 00:07:02,247
White light.
Intensely bright.

122
00:07:02,334 --> 00:07:04,685
And I walked toward it.

123
00:07:04,772 --> 00:07:06,991
There were people, lots of
people gathered around

124
00:07:07,078 --> 00:07:09,080
reaching out
with their hands to me.

125
00:07:09,167 --> 00:07:12,432
There was a woman--
A woman who knew you.

126
00:07:14,477 --> 00:07:15,522
Your mother.

127
00:07:15,609 --> 00:07:17,828
Jane, I'm not impressed.

128
00:07:17,915 --> 00:07:21,919
I told you my mother
died when I was a girl.

129
00:07:22,006 --> 00:07:25,923
Well, did you tell me
that she gave you that cross?

130
00:07:26,010 --> 00:07:31,276
You touched it just like that
when I was unconscious.

131
00:07:31,363 --> 00:07:34,410
It's what led her to me.

132
00:07:34,497 --> 00:07:37,631
'And now I can
lead her to you.'

133
00:07:37,718 --> 00:07:40,764
You wanna put me in touch
with my dead mother?

134
00:07:40,851 --> 00:07:44,942
I'm a psychic, Teresa.
That's what I do.

135
00:07:45,029 --> 00:07:47,423
- He doesn't know me.
- He doesn't know who he is.

136
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:49,294
It's called
dissociative fugue--

137
00:07:49,381 --> 00:07:51,296
the temporary loss
of personal identity.

138
00:07:51,383 --> 00:07:54,038
It can last hours, months,
and in rare cases, years.

139
00:07:54,125 --> 00:07:55,518
But he remembers
some things

140
00:07:55,605 --> 00:07:57,607
like he used to be
a fake psychic.

141
00:07:57,694 --> 00:08:00,392
He did a reading on me,
it was a good one.

142
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:02,264
Has he had any
previous traumas?

143
00:08:02,351 --> 00:08:04,222
His wife and daughter
were murdered.

144
00:08:04,309 --> 00:08:07,225
There you go. His subconscious
mind is protecting itself

145
00:08:07,312 --> 00:08:09,532
from further trauma
by blocking out that pain.

146
00:08:09,619 --> 00:08:12,100
As far as Patrick knows,
his family never existed.

147
00:08:12,187 --> 00:08:14,189
Their death is what
brought him to us.

148
00:08:14,276 --> 00:08:15,973
And that's why
he doesn't know you.

149
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:17,627
But you're just
what he needs

150
00:08:17,714 --> 00:08:19,194
to get back on his feet.

151
00:08:22,632 --> 00:08:25,200
- How should we act?
- Normal.

152
00:08:25,287 --> 00:08:27,594
I never know how to act
around Jane normally.

153
00:08:27,681 --> 00:08:30,466
Then you're all set.
Don't talk about his past.

154
00:08:30,553 --> 00:08:33,121
It's important that his memories
come back on their own.

155
00:08:33,208 --> 00:08:35,384
He should still
be in the hospital.

156
00:08:35,471 --> 00:08:38,256
The doctor wants him
in a familiar environment.

157
00:08:38,343 --> 00:08:40,171
Is he gonna help
with the case?

158
00:08:40,258 --> 00:08:42,173
He says he can't wait.

159
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:45,046
- You sure this is a good idea?
- I don't know.

160
00:08:45,133 --> 00:08:48,397
Jane needs something to hold on
to and we're gonna be there.

161
00:08:48,484 --> 00:08:49,964
Where are we with the victim?

162
00:08:50,051 --> 00:08:51,443
According to his co-workers

163
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,184
Paul Satterfield
was a legend.

164
00:08:53,271 --> 00:08:55,883
The old guard respected him,
the younger guys idolized him.

165
00:08:55,970 --> 00:08:57,493
Satterfield left behind
a widow, Diane.

166
00:08:57,580 --> 00:08:59,016
They were together
for 16 years.

167
00:08:59,103 --> 00:09:00,540
She's coming by to talk.

168
00:09:00,627 --> 00:09:02,542
- Anything from the crime scene?
- No trace evidence.

169
00:09:02,629 --> 00:09:04,065
All the EMT'S
and firefighters

170
00:09:04,152 --> 00:09:07,329
trying to rescue him,
they contaminated everything.

171
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:09,374
Okay, I vote we skip
the awkward introductions

172
00:09:09,461 --> 00:09:11,855
because you all know me,
and in one glance

173
00:09:11,942 --> 00:09:14,945
I know the same about you
as the day I forgot you.

174
00:09:15,032 --> 00:09:16,512
You gave us
a scare yesterday.

175
00:09:16,599 --> 00:09:18,558
Well, not my intention,
I assure you.

176
00:09:21,517 --> 00:09:25,129
Well, we're just glad
you pulled through.

177
00:09:25,216 --> 00:09:26,261
Thanks, Bigsby.

178
00:09:27,654 --> 00:09:29,873
Rigsby.
It's Wayne Rigsby.

179
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:31,614
Wayne.

180
00:09:31,701 --> 00:09:35,226
We're just working the case,
so feel free to jump in anytime.

181
00:09:35,313 --> 00:09:38,882
Okay. Your armed robber--

182
00:09:38,969 --> 00:09:41,015
desperate amateur.
Why work alone?

183
00:09:41,102 --> 00:09:43,844
Because he doesn't know
any other criminals.

184
00:09:43,931 --> 00:09:46,107
The Lucha Libremask
projects the correct attitude.

185
00:09:46,194 --> 00:09:48,718
But look..
Look what he is wearing.

186
00:09:48,805 --> 00:09:51,025
- Khakis and a fitted sweater.
- Jane--

187
00:09:51,112 --> 00:09:53,070
No wonder the guard
tried to shoot him.

188
00:09:53,157 --> 00:09:54,898
The closest a man
should ever come

189
00:09:54,985 --> 00:09:57,422
to touching it is helping
a woman out of one.

190
00:09:57,509 --> 00:10:00,338
Jane, another team is
handling the ATM lobby hit.

191
00:10:00,425 --> 00:10:01,644
We got the dead fireman.

192
00:10:03,298 --> 00:10:05,692
Got it.

193
00:10:05,779 --> 00:10:07,955
[Cho]
'The victim was featured
in a local newscast'

194
00:10:08,042 --> 00:10:09,434
'the day of his murder.'

195
00:10:09,521 --> 00:10:11,132
I pulled a copy.

196
00:10:11,219 --> 00:10:13,090
We might find
something useful in it.

197
00:10:13,177 --> 00:10:15,876
[male on TV]
'A dramatic rescue
before the light of dawn'

198
00:10:15,963 --> 00:10:18,226
'firefighter Paul Satterfield
risked it all'

199
00:10:18,313 --> 00:10:23,057
'to pull an unconscious man from
his burning house in Wilton.'

200
00:10:23,144 --> 00:10:25,407
'Both men barely escaped
with their lives'

201
00:10:25,494 --> 00:10:28,976
'when a dramatic flashover
exploded behind them.'

202
00:10:29,063 --> 00:10:31,152
'Officials believe the fire
spread from the chimney'

203
00:10:31,239 --> 00:10:33,458
'of a two-story home
in Wilton.'

204
00:10:33,545 --> 00:10:35,025
'was treated
for smoke inhalation'

205
00:10:35,112 --> 00:10:36,984
'and released
from the hospital.'

206
00:10:37,071 --> 00:10:39,943
'This isn't the first time we've
featured Paul Satterfield'

207
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:41,336
'on this news cast...'

208
00:10:41,423 --> 00:10:42,990
Where's Satterfield's partner?

209
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:45,514
You always enter a burning
structure with two firefighters.

210
00:10:45,601 --> 00:10:47,951
Who led the victim
into that death trap alone.?

211
00:10:48,038 --> 00:10:50,171
Go and find out.
Take Jane.

212
00:10:50,258 --> 00:10:51,651
Me?

213
00:10:51,738 --> 00:10:53,435
Whoever killed Satterfield
tried to kill you.

214
00:10:53,522 --> 00:10:55,785
Oh, you wanna give him
another try at that?

215
00:10:55,872 --> 00:10:58,483
You'll be okay.
Rigsby will be with you.

216
00:10:58,570 --> 00:11:02,139
Why is Teresa insisting that
I meet these firefighters?

217
00:11:02,226 --> 00:11:03,663
Well, you might
recognize the killer.

218
00:11:03,750 --> 00:11:05,229
Well, it's doubtful.

219
00:11:05,316 --> 00:11:07,536
I was attacked in the dark
and held underwater.

220
00:11:07,623 --> 00:11:09,799
Isn't it likely that
he took me from behind?

221
00:11:09,886 --> 00:11:11,322
Yeah, probably.

222
00:11:11,409 --> 00:11:13,629
But you're still good
at sussing out guilty parties.

223
00:11:13,716 --> 00:11:16,850
Huh.
How do I do that exactly?

224
00:11:16,937 --> 00:11:19,504
Uh, it's hard to say.
Causing trouble, mostly.

225
00:11:19,591 --> 00:11:21,332
And you don't mind?

226
00:11:21,419 --> 00:11:23,857
No, we're a team.
We help each other out.

227
00:11:23,944 --> 00:11:25,336
Oh.

228
00:11:25,423 --> 00:11:27,077
Well, in that case, Wayne,

229
00:11:27,164 --> 00:11:29,384
what's the best way in
with the luscious redhead?

230
00:11:29,471 --> 00:11:30,951
Van Pelt?

231
00:11:31,038 --> 00:11:33,475
Clearly you two had a thing,
and she dumped you

232
00:11:33,562 --> 00:11:37,174
but maybe you can share a tip
on how to open the cookie jar.

233
00:11:37,261 --> 00:11:38,523
Get some, uh..

234
00:11:38,610 --> 00:11:40,656
...tasty gingersnaps?

235
00:11:40,743 --> 00:11:42,484
Oh.
You want a tip?

236
00:11:42,571 --> 00:11:44,051
[elevator bell dings]

237
00:11:44,138 --> 00:11:45,052
Back off.

238
00:11:50,231 --> 00:11:51,711
I thought we
were a team.

239
00:11:54,409 --> 00:11:56,367
I'm sorry for your loss,
Mrs. Satterfield.

240
00:11:56,454 --> 00:11:58,500
- You are, are you?
- Yes, ma'am.

241
00:11:58,587 --> 00:12:00,937
I hold firefighters
in the highest regard.

242
00:12:01,024 --> 00:12:04,593
Even ones who die
in a knife fight?

243
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:06,595
Hell, even I didn't see
that one coming.

244
00:12:06,682 --> 00:12:08,902
Oh. You mind?

245
00:12:10,555 --> 00:12:11,556
It's not allowed.

246
00:12:16,474 --> 00:12:18,955
Did your husband
have any enemies?

247
00:12:19,042 --> 00:12:22,002
God, no.
Paul Satterfield was a hero.

248
00:12:22,089 --> 00:12:23,612
You didn't hear?

249
00:12:23,699 --> 00:12:24,874
You will.

250
00:12:27,659 --> 00:12:28,748
Where's your wedding ring?

251
00:12:30,619 --> 00:12:34,405
Oh, I'm having it
melted for cash.

252
00:12:34,492 --> 00:12:38,148
No, not really.
At least not for cash anyway.

253
00:12:38,235 --> 00:12:40,716
Having it resized
to fit my middle finger.

254
00:12:40,803 --> 00:12:42,283
Think of it
as a statement.

255
00:12:42,370 --> 00:12:45,199
Clearly you don't mind being
considered a murder suspect.

256
00:12:45,286 --> 00:12:47,114
Consider me
whatever you want.

257
00:12:47,201 --> 00:12:49,116
You know?

258
00:12:49,203 --> 00:12:51,596
You're wasting your time.

259
00:12:51,683 --> 00:12:53,120
I was working last night.

260
00:12:53,207 --> 00:12:54,599
Yeah? Where?

261
00:12:54,686 --> 00:12:56,427
Suicide prevention hotline.

262
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,344
It's true.

263
00:13:00,431 --> 00:13:01,693
Two minutes with me

264
00:13:01,781 --> 00:13:04,218
and your life
ain't looking so bad.

265
00:13:04,305 --> 00:13:06,960
You sure I can't
have a smoke?

266
00:13:07,047 --> 00:13:11,225
[indistinct conversations]

267
00:13:11,312 --> 00:13:14,968
Gather around, please.
These men are with CBI.

268
00:13:15,055 --> 00:13:18,014
We're gonna do everything we can
to help them out for Paul.

269
00:13:18,101 --> 00:13:19,581
Thank you, captain.

270
00:13:19,668 --> 00:13:22,236
My name is Agent Rigsby.
This is Patrick Jane.

271
00:13:22,323 --> 00:13:25,021
I know that you're here
today are on your day off

272
00:13:25,108 --> 00:13:27,154
and I wanna thank
you all for coming.

273
00:13:27,241 --> 00:13:29,199
I spent a lot of time
working arson

274
00:13:29,286 --> 00:13:31,245
which is why I know
that Paul Satterfield

275
00:13:31,332 --> 00:13:34,161
was hung out to dry yesterday
during that house fire.

276
00:13:34,248 --> 00:13:35,727
- What are you suggesting?
- Doesn't matter.

277
00:13:35,815 --> 00:13:38,078
- I got what I need.
- You recognize the killer?

278
00:13:38,165 --> 00:13:40,341
No, but I've seen enough
to identify the killer.

279
00:13:40,428 --> 00:13:42,212
Or, more specifically

280
00:13:42,299 --> 00:13:44,998
your mascot here
has smelled enough.

281
00:13:45,085 --> 00:13:47,174
- What's his name?
- Hoser.

282
00:13:47,261 --> 00:13:48,653
Naturally. Uh, may I?

283
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:49,741
Sure.

284
00:13:49,829 --> 00:13:51,526
Come on, Hoser.

285
00:13:51,613 --> 00:13:54,398
We've all heard of dogs
that have found their way home

286
00:13:54,485 --> 00:13:55,965
from thousands of miles away.

287
00:13:56,052 --> 00:13:59,882
That's because these animals
have a sixth sense.

288
00:13:59,969 --> 00:14:02,363
Natural disasters,
the presence of spirits

289
00:14:02,450 --> 00:14:05,366
some of them can even
detect cancer.

290
00:14:05,453 --> 00:14:08,021
- That's a bunch of crap.
- Really?

291
00:14:08,108 --> 00:14:10,066
An Atlanta woman
credits her dog Floyd Henry

292
00:14:10,153 --> 00:14:13,287
for pawing a cancerous tumor
which saved her life.

293
00:14:13,374 --> 00:14:14,549
Why do I remember that?

294
00:14:16,768 --> 00:14:19,902
In any case, on my command,
our canine friend here

295
00:14:19,989 --> 00:14:22,252
will use his
extrasensory perception

296
00:14:22,339 --> 00:14:26,387
to sniff out
a killer's guilt.

297
00:14:26,474 --> 00:14:28,389
And I do believe
he's found it.

298
00:14:28,476 --> 00:14:31,174
Hoser, go get the killer.

299
00:14:33,568 --> 00:14:34,874
- Huh.
- Hey.

300
00:14:37,702 --> 00:14:39,617
- What's this?
- Smoked kipper.

301
00:14:39,704 --> 00:14:41,271
Also known as red herring.

302
00:14:41,358 --> 00:14:43,317
[laughing]
I found it in your kitchen.

303
00:14:45,449 --> 00:14:46,494
[chuckling]
What?

304
00:14:48,148 --> 00:14:49,192
What's not funny?

305
00:14:49,279 --> 00:14:51,542
What's going on here?

306
00:14:51,629 --> 00:14:52,848
You said I cause trouble.

307
00:14:52,935 --> 00:14:54,197
Yeah, to help
the investigation.

308
00:14:54,284 --> 00:14:56,243
That guy's the paramedic
that saved your life.

309
00:14:56,330 --> 00:15:00,029
- Oh, well, thank you very much.
- No problem.

310
00:15:00,116 --> 00:15:02,727
- Are we done here?
- Not by a mile.

311
00:15:02,814 --> 00:15:06,688
When Hoser here was, uh,
pouncing on my rescuer

312
00:15:06,775 --> 00:15:09,560
I couldn't help but notice
a profound sense of relief

313
00:15:09,647 --> 00:15:11,780
from..

314
00:15:11,867 --> 00:15:13,521
...you, sir.

315
00:15:15,218 --> 00:15:16,916
What's your relationship
with the deceased?

316
00:15:17,003 --> 00:15:19,005
Um, not much.

317
00:15:19,092 --> 00:15:20,920
No sad feelings,
no wedding ring.

318
00:15:21,007 --> 00:15:22,660
[sniffs]

319
00:15:22,747 --> 00:15:24,358
That's ladies' perfume

320
00:15:24,445 --> 00:15:27,056
with a top note of orange
mixed with bitter almond.

321
00:15:27,143 --> 00:15:30,886
That's not a young
woman's fragrance.

322
00:15:30,973 --> 00:15:33,802
Were you sleeping
with the victim's wife?

323
00:15:33,889 --> 00:15:36,065
That's offensive.
Firefighters are a brotherhood.

324
00:15:36,152 --> 00:15:37,632
Wives are off-limits.

325
00:15:37,719 --> 00:15:38,938
Well, tell him that.

326
00:15:39,025 --> 00:15:40,548
It's time
for you to go.

327
00:15:40,635 --> 00:15:43,420
Huh, Larkin?

328
00:15:43,507 --> 00:15:45,683
- It's time for you to go.
- It's okay, cap.

329
00:15:49,035 --> 00:15:50,862
It's all gonna
come out anyway.

330
00:15:54,127 --> 00:15:56,999
What he said is true.

331
00:15:57,086 --> 00:15:58,958
I was having an affair
with Paul's wife.

332
00:16:02,526 --> 00:16:04,267
How was that?

333
00:16:04,354 --> 00:16:05,834
Yeah.
Just like the old you.

334
00:16:13,450 --> 00:16:15,322
Heard you riled 'em up
in there.

335
00:16:15,409 --> 00:16:16,845
Sounds like
the Jane I know.

336
00:16:16,932 --> 00:16:17,889
Hmm.

337
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:22,982
Is anything coming
back to you?

338
00:16:23,069 --> 00:16:25,680
Kids prefer cheese
over fried green spinach.

339
00:16:25,767 --> 00:16:27,638
[chuckling]
Come again?

340
00:16:27,725 --> 00:16:30,554
It's an acronym for
the seven levels of taxonomy.

341
00:16:30,641 --> 00:16:33,253
Kingdom, phylum,
class, order

342
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:35,995
family, genus,
species.

343
00:16:36,082 --> 00:16:37,344
Impressive.

344
00:16:37,431 --> 00:16:39,389
I can also name
the geological time periods

345
00:16:39,476 --> 00:16:41,043
the world's longest rivers
by size

346
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:42,697
and the complete
works of Shakespeare

347
00:16:42,784 --> 00:16:44,264
in chronological order.

348
00:16:44,351 --> 00:16:46,353
So the memory palace
is intact.

349
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:48,442
I told you
about the memory palace?

350
00:16:48,529 --> 00:16:49,617
We're friends.

351
00:16:51,488 --> 00:16:53,664
The doctor says it's
your emotional memories

352
00:16:53,751 --> 00:16:55,144
that are tripping you up.

353
00:16:55,231 --> 00:16:57,146
Has anything
come back to you?

354
00:16:57,233 --> 00:16:58,191
Anything at all?

355
00:16:58,278 --> 00:16:59,192
No.

356
00:17:00,541 --> 00:17:02,108
What do you remember
about this?

357
00:17:02,195 --> 00:17:03,848
My wedding ring?

358
00:17:03,935 --> 00:17:05,894
No better way
to gain a woman's trust

359
00:17:05,981 --> 00:17:09,463
than to wear a wedding ring,
except maybe buy a dog.

360
00:17:09,550 --> 00:17:12,509
Okay, so you wear a wedding ring
to get over on women?

361
00:17:12,596 --> 00:17:14,250
Worked on you.

362
00:17:14,337 --> 00:17:18,385
You remember the house fire
that we saw on the newscast?

363
00:17:18,472 --> 00:17:20,909
It was the only call
that the victim responded to

364
00:17:20,996 --> 00:17:22,737
the day that
he was murdered.

365
00:17:22,824 --> 00:17:24,739
I'm gonna have Cho
look at the house.

366
00:17:24,826 --> 00:17:26,001
Go with him.

367
00:17:26,088 --> 00:17:27,785
It's his turn
to babysit me now?

368
00:17:27,872 --> 00:17:29,787
Well, somebody has to.

369
00:17:29,874 --> 00:17:31,659
Pleasant weather.

370
00:17:31,746 --> 00:17:33,704
Has it been like
this all week?

371
00:17:33,791 --> 00:17:35,445
I guess. Why?

372
00:17:35,532 --> 00:17:39,797
Big elephants can always
understand small elephants

373
00:17:39,884 --> 00:17:42,452
because, Teresa, because.

374
00:17:42,539 --> 00:17:44,889
Oh. Oh, I get it.
It's another acronym.

375
00:17:44,976 --> 00:17:46,282
Mm.

376
00:17:49,155 --> 00:17:50,417
Were you at the firehouse

377
00:17:50,504 --> 00:17:52,201
the night the victim
was murdered?

378
00:17:52,288 --> 00:17:54,986
I left early to go
to the bachelor party.

379
00:17:55,074 --> 00:17:56,075
Were you with anyone?

380
00:17:56,162 --> 00:17:57,598
No.

381
00:17:57,685 --> 00:17:59,121
So you could have
circled back?

382
00:17:59,208 --> 00:18:01,732
I could have,
but I didn't.

383
00:18:03,430 --> 00:18:05,301
Why were you sleeping
with Mrs. Satterfield?

384
00:18:07,216 --> 00:18:08,565
It felt good.

385
00:18:08,652 --> 00:18:10,350
Good enough to want
her husband dead?

386
00:18:10,437 --> 00:18:12,134
I didn't want him dead.

387
00:18:12,221 --> 00:18:13,440
Oh, no?

388
00:18:13,527 --> 00:18:15,181
Well, I checked
yesterday's logs.

389
00:18:15,268 --> 00:18:16,921
You were
Satterfield's partner.

390
00:18:17,008 --> 00:18:19,707
You let him go upstairs
without backup, alone.

391
00:18:19,794 --> 00:18:24,190
I was the one who was
left without backup, okay?

392
00:18:24,277 --> 00:18:27,584
We live and dieby SOG's.
do you know what those are?

393
00:18:27,671 --> 00:18:29,064
Standard operating guidelines.

394
00:18:29,151 --> 00:18:30,544
Yeah.

395
00:18:30,631 --> 00:18:32,807
And we were conducting
a standard left-hand search

396
00:18:32,894 --> 00:18:35,201
of a fully involved house

397
00:18:35,288 --> 00:18:38,117
when Paul decides to run
upstairs and find the homeowner.

398
00:18:38,204 --> 00:18:39,814
Why didn't you go with him?

399
00:18:39,901 --> 00:18:44,035
He left me in a smoke-filled
room with zero visibility.

400
00:18:44,123 --> 00:18:47,430
I couldn't see my hand
in front of my face

401
00:18:47,517 --> 00:18:49,563
much less
which way he ran.

402
00:18:51,869 --> 00:18:53,915
That's the way it was
with Paul.

403
00:18:54,002 --> 00:18:57,223
He could break
every rule

404
00:18:57,310 --> 00:18:59,529
leave his
partner in danger

405
00:18:59,616 --> 00:19:01,052
and still
he's the hero.

406
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:02,837
So why didn't you
tell the captain?

407
00:19:02,924 --> 00:19:04,404
Paul was good press.

408
00:19:08,973 --> 00:19:12,325
Look, I feel bad
that he's gone.

409
00:19:12,412 --> 00:19:14,327
But I'll tell you what.

410
00:19:14,414 --> 00:19:16,894
I'm glad that I don't
have to count on him

411
00:19:16,981 --> 00:19:19,158
to cover my back
at the next fire.

412
00:19:25,338 --> 00:19:27,078
You take this side
of the street.

413
00:19:27,166 --> 00:19:29,124
Find out if the neighbors
saw the victim

414
00:19:29,211 --> 00:19:30,908
interacting with anyone
suspicious during the fire.

415
00:19:30,995 --> 00:19:33,259
You want me to go
door to door?

416
00:19:33,346 --> 00:19:35,261
You do it all the time.

417
00:19:35,348 --> 00:19:37,045
It's one of the more
interesting things

418
00:19:37,132 --> 00:19:38,089
about being a consultant.

419
00:19:38,177 --> 00:19:40,179
[chuckling]

420
00:19:40,266 --> 00:19:42,137
You're a clever one,
Mr. Cho.

421
00:19:42,224 --> 00:19:43,921
And I'll do your bidding

422
00:19:44,008 --> 00:19:47,098
but first I wanna take a look
at this burned-out house.

423
00:19:47,186 --> 00:19:49,884
When can I go inside?
I need to get in there.

424
00:19:51,146 --> 00:19:52,626
CBI.
What's going on here?

425
00:19:52,713 --> 00:19:54,671
They won't let me
inside my house.

426
00:19:54,758 --> 00:19:56,325
It's a standard
arson investigation.

427
00:19:56,412 --> 00:19:58,762
- We'll call him when it's over.
- This is crazy.

428
00:19:58,849 --> 00:20:00,808
Why would I
set fire to my house?

429
00:20:00,895 --> 00:20:02,505
Everything is in there.

430
00:20:02,592 --> 00:20:04,638
- My kid's childhood--
- No one's accusing you, sir.

431
00:20:04,725 --> 00:20:07,467
I know you.
I saw you on the TV.

432
00:20:07,554 --> 00:20:09,817
The fireman saved your life.
Patrick Jane.

433
00:20:09,904 --> 00:20:12,646
Yeah. Tom Wilcox.

434
00:20:12,733 --> 00:20:15,910
I almost died yesterday, too,
but, uh, no news cameras.

435
00:20:15,997 --> 00:20:18,826
Did you know the man
who saved you was killed?

436
00:20:18,913 --> 00:20:22,699
Yeah, I read it
in the paper.

437
00:20:22,786 --> 00:20:24,745
I didn't get a chance
to thank him.

438
00:20:24,832 --> 00:20:27,182
Did you see the victim
in conflict with anyone?

439
00:20:27,269 --> 00:20:29,706
No, I was unconscious
the whole time.

440
00:20:29,793 --> 00:20:32,535
Me, too. The guy put
my head under the water.

441
00:20:32,622 --> 00:20:34,755
How did you black out?

442
00:20:34,842 --> 00:20:36,409
When the fire
jumped the fireplace

443
00:20:36,496 --> 00:20:39,194
I ran upstairs and grabbed
all the valuables that I could

444
00:20:39,281 --> 00:20:41,762
but the smoke was too thick,
and I couldn't breathe.

445
00:20:41,849 --> 00:20:43,459
Well, we both
cheated death.

446
00:20:43,546 --> 00:20:45,156
That, that's just..
That's remarkable.

447
00:20:45,244 --> 00:20:47,202
You know, we should
get t-shirts made up.

448
00:20:47,289 --> 00:20:48,725
Well, I'm glad
that you're happy

449
00:20:48,812 --> 00:20:50,597
but I lost
everything that I own.

450
00:20:50,684 --> 00:20:53,904
I got my wife and my daughter
in a motel I can't afford.

451
00:20:53,991 --> 00:20:55,732
Nothing but the clothes
on our backs.

452
00:20:55,819 --> 00:20:57,995
You can ask your insurance
company for an advance?

453
00:21:01,303 --> 00:21:03,087
My house isn't insured.

454
00:21:03,174 --> 00:21:05,655
I mean it was, but..

455
00:21:05,742 --> 00:21:07,309
I'm a savings
and loan officer

456
00:21:07,396 --> 00:21:09,311
and ever since
the banks collapsed

457
00:21:09,398 --> 00:21:10,921
I've been out of work.

458
00:21:11,008 --> 00:21:13,141
Okay. Well, let's forget
about the t-shirts, then.

459
00:21:13,228 --> 00:21:16,492
Mr. Cho, let's say we give
this poor man a ride back.

460
00:21:16,579 --> 00:21:17,580
Come on.

461
00:21:20,627 --> 00:21:23,325
- Okay, thanks.
- Hey, boss?

462
00:21:23,412 --> 00:21:24,848
On Rigsby's advice,
I went through

463
00:21:24,935 --> 00:21:26,372
Mrs. Satterfield's
credit reports.

464
00:21:26,459 --> 00:21:27,677
Take a look at this.

465
00:21:30,724 --> 00:21:33,422
Looks like our grieving widow
had a lot to celebrate.

466
00:21:33,509 --> 00:21:34,945
Yeah.

467
00:21:35,032 --> 00:21:37,034
I'm thinking about using
that life insurance money

468
00:21:37,121 --> 00:21:38,732
to redo my kitchen.

469
00:21:38,819 --> 00:21:41,691
No offense, Mrs. Satterfield,
but you're quite brazen.

470
00:21:41,778 --> 00:21:44,477
Which means you're either
innocent or stupid.

471
00:21:44,564 --> 00:21:47,044
Well, she doubled down on her
husband's life insurance policy

472
00:21:47,131 --> 00:21:48,611
a month before his murder.

473
00:21:48,698 --> 00:21:50,047
I'm going with stupid.

474
00:21:50,134 --> 00:21:52,223
Wouldn't be the first time
a cheating wife

475
00:21:52,311 --> 00:21:54,095
arranged for
her husband's death.

476
00:21:54,182 --> 00:21:55,966
You think I was unfaithful?

477
00:21:56,053 --> 00:21:58,055
You were sleeping
with another man.

478
00:21:58,142 --> 00:22:00,884
Paul knew about my affair.

479
00:22:00,971 --> 00:22:02,625
Truth is, he was relieved.

480
00:22:02,712 --> 00:22:04,845
I stopped begging
for his attention.

481
00:22:04,932 --> 00:22:07,238
He could never
give me what I wanted.

482
00:22:07,326 --> 00:22:10,894
- Which was?
- A marriage.

483
00:22:10,981 --> 00:22:13,723
He left ours years ago.

484
00:22:13,810 --> 00:22:15,769
My husband was a junkie.

485
00:22:15,856 --> 00:22:17,858
- Heroin?
- Adrenaline.

486
00:22:17,945 --> 00:22:19,599
Couldn't get enough of it.

487
00:22:19,686 --> 00:22:22,210
Drinking in all that glory

488
00:22:22,297 --> 00:22:23,951
dancing in the fire.

489
00:22:24,038 --> 00:22:27,346
How do you compete with
the high of being a hero?

490
00:22:28,999 --> 00:22:30,827
Why didn't you just leave him?

491
00:22:30,914 --> 00:22:32,351
And what kind of a settlement

492
00:22:32,438 --> 00:22:34,353
am I gonna get
from a firefighter?

493
00:22:34,440 --> 00:22:37,878
I upped the policy because his
death was just a matter of time.

494
00:22:37,965 --> 00:22:42,709
Every day, a race towards
the edge of a cliff.

495
00:22:42,796 --> 00:22:45,712
I bet getting stabbed was
the biggest rush of his life.

496
00:22:56,462 --> 00:22:57,637
Thanks for the ride.

497
00:22:57,724 --> 00:23:01,162
It's, uh, it's been
a tough day.

498
00:23:01,249 --> 00:23:02,381
Hi, dad!

499
00:23:02,468 --> 00:23:04,339
Hey, baby.

500
00:23:04,426 --> 00:23:06,080
Who are your friends?

501
00:23:06,167 --> 00:23:07,864
I'm Agent Cho.
This is Patrick Jane.

502
00:23:07,951 --> 00:23:09,692
We're investigating the murder
of Paul Satterfield.

503
00:23:09,779 --> 00:23:11,955
I was just reading
about him in the paper.

504
00:23:12,042 --> 00:23:13,653
'So sad.'

505
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:15,698
'Lulu would be without
a father right now'

506
00:23:15,785 --> 00:23:17,004
'if it weren't for him.'

507
00:23:17,091 --> 00:23:18,919
Do you know anything
about the funeral?

508
00:23:19,006 --> 00:23:20,660
Uh, we'd like to be there.

509
00:23:20,747 --> 00:23:23,010
Well, they usually have that
information in the story.

510
00:23:23,097 --> 00:23:24,620
Let me have a look here.

511
00:23:24,707 --> 00:23:26,492
Oh, this clown again.

512
00:23:26,579 --> 00:23:29,364
He's stealing a lot of press
from our fallen hero.

513
00:23:29,451 --> 00:23:31,061
Did you find my dolls?

514
00:23:31,148 --> 00:23:32,802
I'm afraid not, honey.

515
00:23:32,889 --> 00:23:35,326
I still haven't been able
to get inside our house.

516
00:23:36,589 --> 00:23:38,199
Well, not to fear.

517
00:23:38,286 --> 00:23:40,984
Mister Cho here and I
will go back to your home

518
00:23:41,071 --> 00:23:44,292
and see what we can find
to make your life easier.

519
00:23:44,379 --> 00:23:47,556
- Yes!
- No, you don't have to do that.

520
00:23:47,643 --> 00:23:50,429
- It's okay.
- I miss my dolls.

521
00:23:50,516 --> 00:23:52,256
Not for long.

522
00:23:52,343 --> 00:23:53,736
Thank you, both of you.

523
00:23:53,823 --> 00:23:55,303
Shall we?

524
00:24:01,875 --> 00:24:03,311
What's your play here?

525
00:24:03,398 --> 00:24:04,878
I don't know what you mean.

526
00:24:04,965 --> 00:24:06,445
What are you trying to do?

527
00:24:06,532 --> 00:24:08,969
Bring a ray of hope
to a family, I suppose.

528
00:24:09,056 --> 00:24:11,362
And help my friends
solve a crime.

529
00:24:15,932 --> 00:24:17,760
I don't buy it.

530
00:24:17,847 --> 00:24:20,154
You don't help us
because you like us.

531
00:24:21,677 --> 00:24:23,723
I don't like you?

532
00:24:23,810 --> 00:24:25,507
Well, you don't
not like us.

533
00:24:25,594 --> 00:24:28,684
But you have deeper reasons
for helping people.

534
00:24:28,771 --> 00:24:31,513
Without those reasons,
you're a hustler.

535
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:32,601
So what's your play?

536
00:24:34,473 --> 00:24:37,171
You're wrong about me,
Mr. Cho.

537
00:24:37,258 --> 00:24:39,042
I do like you.

538
00:24:39,129 --> 00:24:41,828
And I can assure you I wanna
help that little girl

539
00:24:41,915 --> 00:24:43,090
find her lost doll.

540
00:24:44,395 --> 00:24:45,396
Okay.

541
00:25:02,239 --> 00:25:03,589
What's wrong?

542
00:25:03,676 --> 00:25:08,245
This doll.
It reminds me of something.

543
00:25:08,332 --> 00:25:11,248
I can't, I can't quite..

544
00:25:12,989 --> 00:25:14,208
It's your family?

545
00:25:16,776 --> 00:25:18,038
Yeah, my family.

546
00:25:21,607 --> 00:25:23,478
I can't believe I..

547
00:25:26,176 --> 00:25:27,656
How could I forget my family?

548
00:25:31,225 --> 00:25:32,661
I'm sorry.

549
00:25:36,230 --> 00:25:39,799
Could you give me a minute,
please, Mr. Cho?

550
00:25:39,886 --> 00:25:41,540
Sure. I'll wait outside.

551
00:25:54,509 --> 00:25:57,381
[dial clicking]

552
00:26:12,266 --> 00:26:14,007
[knock on door]

553
00:26:14,094 --> 00:26:15,095
Jane?

554
00:26:18,185 --> 00:26:19,360
[door squeaking]

555
00:26:32,503 --> 00:26:35,594
Okay, if he does show up,
please call us right away.

556
00:26:35,681 --> 00:26:36,682
Thank you.

557
00:26:37,813 --> 00:26:39,206
He's not at the hospital.

558
00:26:39,293 --> 00:26:42,078
I'm starting to get worried.
He has no money or ID.

559
00:26:42,165 --> 00:26:44,167
Uh, yeah, he has money.

560
00:26:44,254 --> 00:26:46,909
Found this in my wallet
when we got back.

561
00:26:46,996 --> 00:26:48,345
"I owe you 60 bucks?"

562
00:26:48,432 --> 00:26:49,825
Yet I'm $63 light.

563
00:26:49,912 --> 00:26:51,392
He wasn't even honest
about that.

564
00:26:51,479 --> 00:26:53,089
Let's start calling
local cab companies

565
00:26:53,176 --> 00:26:55,875
see if any of the drivers
remember giving him a ride.

566
00:26:55,962 --> 00:26:57,920
It's my fault.
I should know better.

567
00:26:58,007 --> 00:27:00,531
So should I.
I'm the one who got him out.

568
00:27:00,619 --> 00:27:02,359
We were all marks today.

569
00:27:02,446 --> 00:27:03,534
Not me.

570
00:27:05,928 --> 00:27:07,277
What? He didn't con me.

571
00:27:07,364 --> 00:27:09,540
Only because he wants
to date you.

572
00:27:09,628 --> 00:27:11,107
Really?

573
00:27:11,194 --> 00:27:12,369
The death of Jane's family

574
00:27:12,456 --> 00:27:13,893
made him a better person.

575
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:16,373
That person was always
inside of him.

576
00:27:16,460 --> 00:27:18,549
The murder just
brought it out.

577
00:27:18,637 --> 00:27:20,726
Rigsby.
Slow down. Who is this?

578
00:27:23,163 --> 00:27:24,643
Are you drunk?

579
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:26,209
I do not do
spiritual readings.

580
00:27:26,296 --> 00:27:27,776
You must have
the wrong number.

581
00:27:27,863 --> 00:27:29,822
- No, stop! No, stop!
- Don't hang up!

582
00:27:29,909 --> 00:27:32,738
Hello?
Where are you calling from?

583
00:27:32,825 --> 00:27:37,830
Okay. I'm being pulled
in this direction.

584
00:27:37,917 --> 00:27:41,921
There's an older woman, an aunt
or a cousin coming through.

585
00:27:42,008 --> 00:27:45,228
There's a "K" connection.

586
00:27:45,315 --> 00:27:47,753
A "K" connection
somewhere over here.

587
00:27:49,711 --> 00:27:52,845
My mother's name
was Kate.

588
00:27:52,932 --> 00:27:55,195
Twins. She's saying
something about--

589
00:27:55,282 --> 00:27:56,849
What do you know
about twins?

590
00:27:56,936 --> 00:27:58,938
I'm a Gemini.

591
00:27:59,025 --> 00:28:01,680
That's your mother's way
of letting you know she's here.

592
00:28:01,767 --> 00:28:04,421
She's letting me know it's very
important that you know.

593
00:28:04,508 --> 00:28:06,554
'That you know
this is her opportunity'

594
00:28:06,641 --> 00:28:07,903
'to make a connection.'

595
00:28:07,990 --> 00:28:11,341
This is her chance
to come through.

596
00:28:11,428 --> 00:28:12,821
[voice breaking]
Okay.

597
00:28:12,908 --> 00:28:14,693
Okay.

598
00:28:14,780 --> 00:28:17,521
Your mother's saying..

599
00:28:17,608 --> 00:28:20,133
...she was killed.

600
00:28:20,220 --> 00:28:23,658
What I mean by that is..

601
00:28:23,745 --> 00:28:26,661
hers was not
a natural passing.

602
00:28:26,748 --> 00:28:27,923
Is that right?

603
00:28:28,010 --> 00:28:29,620
It was a car crash.

604
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:31,666
Okay. I'm sorry.
Okay.

605
00:28:31,753 --> 00:28:34,364
[inhales and exhales deeply]

606
00:28:34,451 --> 00:28:36,932
That's how you lost her.
In a car crash.

607
00:28:37,019 --> 00:28:38,325
'That's what you tell people.'

608
00:28:38,412 --> 00:28:40,501
"I lost my mother
in a car crash.

609
00:28:40,588 --> 00:28:43,243
I lost her. I lost her."

610
00:28:43,330 --> 00:28:47,160
But what you're forgetting
is that she lost you, too.

611
00:28:47,247 --> 00:28:50,206
'There is no greater loss
than a parent's loss of a child'

612
00:28:50,293 --> 00:28:51,512
'but it's okay.'

613
00:28:51,599 --> 00:28:53,296
'She wants you
to know it's okay'

614
00:28:53,383 --> 00:28:55,429
'cause she's here with you.

615
00:28:55,516 --> 00:28:57,736
'She's here tonight,
and she's telling me'

616
00:28:57,823 --> 00:29:00,564
'that she wants a hug.'

617
00:29:00,651 --> 00:29:02,610
A huge hug.
Come here.

618
00:29:02,697 --> 00:29:04,307
Come here.

619
00:29:04,394 --> 00:29:06,657
come on, please.
Come on.

620
00:29:06,745 --> 00:29:07,702
[applauding]

621
00:29:07,789 --> 00:29:09,486
It's okay.

622
00:29:09,573 --> 00:29:10,749
Oh, that's okay.

623
00:29:12,794 --> 00:29:14,143
I hate him.

624
00:29:14,230 --> 00:29:15,710
That's not him.

625
00:29:17,756 --> 00:29:19,366
You're very brave.

626
00:29:19,453 --> 00:29:21,281
You have a very
strong connection.

627
00:29:21,368 --> 00:29:23,544
You won't lose that now.

628
00:29:23,631 --> 00:29:26,112
Thank you, folks. Whoo.

629
00:29:26,199 --> 00:29:29,028
[applauding]

630
00:29:34,468 --> 00:29:35,861
Hi.

631
00:29:35,948 --> 00:29:36,992
What are you doing?

632
00:29:37,079 --> 00:29:38,646
What's it look like?
Business.

633
00:29:38,733 --> 00:29:41,344
Excuse me. May I?

634
00:29:41,431 --> 00:29:42,998
This is Rigsby's card.

635
00:29:43,085 --> 00:29:45,784
Yeah, just you know until
I get my own printed up.

636
00:29:45,871 --> 00:29:47,786
You said that you
were dying to help.

637
00:29:47,873 --> 00:29:49,004
This is not helping.

638
00:29:49,091 --> 00:29:50,484
I am.

639
00:29:50,571 --> 00:29:52,965
I'm investigating. I felt bad
about the fish stunt

640
00:29:53,052 --> 00:29:54,314
I pulled on these boys

641
00:29:54,401 --> 00:29:57,317
so, uh, I decided to take
the "B" shift out

642
00:29:57,404 --> 00:29:59,580
for a night on the town.

643
00:29:59,667 --> 00:30:01,321
Make sure they're all
innocent of murder.

644
00:30:01,408 --> 00:30:02,888
Are they?

645
00:30:02,975 --> 00:30:04,628
They're the best of the best!

646
00:30:04,715 --> 00:30:06,413
- Yep! Yep!
- You are the man, paddy!

647
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:08,154
- Thank you! Cheers!
- Cheers!

648
00:30:08,241 --> 00:30:11,722
Oh, he got the last round.
Could you thank him for me?

649
00:30:11,810 --> 00:30:13,681
We're going back
to the hospital now.

650
00:30:13,768 --> 00:30:15,204
I'm gonna take a rain check.

651
00:30:15,291 --> 00:30:16,553
I got another show later.

652
00:30:16,640 --> 00:30:18,381
I'm best with the late crowd.

653
00:30:18,468 --> 00:30:20,253
Are you gonna make me
call backup

654
00:30:20,340 --> 00:30:23,517
to get you out of this bar?
Because I will do that, paddy.

655
00:30:26,215 --> 00:30:28,087
Gentlemen

656
00:30:28,174 --> 00:30:29,436
the best of the best!

657
00:30:29,523 --> 00:30:31,003
- Yeah, buddy!
- Hey, paddy!

658
00:30:31,090 --> 00:30:33,614
- Salute.
- Well said, my man, well said.

659
00:30:33,701 --> 00:30:35,834
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

660
00:30:35,921 --> 00:30:37,357
[rock music]

661
00:30:40,403 --> 00:30:43,189
I got it from here.
Thanks.

662
00:30:43,276 --> 00:30:45,104
I'll see you in the morning.

663
00:30:45,191 --> 00:30:47,106
Uh, no. Actually,
put a fork in it.

664
00:30:48,498 --> 00:30:50,674
I'm done.
No more police work.

665
00:30:50,761 --> 00:30:52,459
I understand.

666
00:30:52,546 --> 00:30:54,853
You can stay in the hospital
until your fine.

667
00:30:54,940 --> 00:30:57,812
What if I don't get fine?

668
00:30:57,899 --> 00:30:59,596
I talked to my
paramedic friends.

669
00:30:59,683 --> 00:31:02,469
All I need to be released
from this hospital

670
00:31:02,556 --> 00:31:05,472
is the signature
from a responsible adult

671
00:31:05,559 --> 00:31:07,909
and I got some
pretty tasty offers tonight.

672
00:31:07,996 --> 00:31:09,606
[laughing]

673
00:31:09,693 --> 00:31:11,130
And, this wedding ring--

674
00:31:11,217 --> 00:31:14,960
I can make you stay,
you know.

675
00:31:15,047 --> 00:31:17,701
It's in my power to hold you
as a material witness

676
00:31:17,788 --> 00:31:19,660
in a murder investigation.

677
00:31:19,747 --> 00:31:21,053
Why would you do that?

678
00:31:22,663 --> 00:31:25,448
You think I can't see
what's going on here?

679
00:31:25,535 --> 00:31:27,233
You people, you're,
you're tiptoeing.

680
00:31:27,320 --> 00:31:30,149
You're dancing around
some forgotten tragedy.

681
00:31:32,499 --> 00:31:34,588
I'm happy now. Just..

682
00:31:36,633 --> 00:31:38,461
Just let me be happy.

683
00:31:40,072 --> 00:31:41,725
Fair enough.

684
00:31:41,812 --> 00:31:44,728
Look, I'll miss you,
but I'll leave you alone, okay?

685
00:31:44,815 --> 00:31:47,340
Thank you, Teresa.
I appreciate it.

686
00:31:52,214 --> 00:31:55,435
Any parting thoughts
on the murder?

687
00:31:55,522 --> 00:31:57,959
Yeah, I figured out
who killed the fireman.

688
00:31:58,046 --> 00:31:59,221
You did? Who?

689
00:31:59,308 --> 00:32:00,353
Jacket left pocket.

690
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:08,578
Kind of obvious,
don't you think?

691
00:32:08,665 --> 00:32:11,016
No.

692
00:32:11,103 --> 00:32:13,148
Should I spell it
out for you?

693
00:32:13,235 --> 00:32:14,845
Yes.

694
00:32:14,933 --> 00:32:17,631
I suppose you need me
to gift wrap the killer, too?

695
00:32:19,067 --> 00:32:20,677
You usually do.

696
00:32:20,764 --> 00:32:21,896
Hmm.

697
00:32:23,724 --> 00:32:26,248
My parting gift to you
I will give tomorrow morning.

698
00:32:26,335 --> 00:32:27,467
Goodnight.

699
00:32:33,038 --> 00:32:34,953
There's room up here.

700
00:32:41,829 --> 00:32:43,048
Moving van, huh?

701
00:32:43,135 --> 00:32:45,311
You have to put
a deposit down on that?

702
00:32:45,398 --> 00:32:46,616
I thought you were broke.

703
00:32:46,703 --> 00:32:49,880
Well, the investigation
is over.

704
00:32:49,968 --> 00:32:51,447
They let me in the house

705
00:32:51,534 --> 00:32:54,320
and I found our
emergency cash, thank god.

706
00:32:55,669 --> 00:32:56,844
And now you're going away?

707
00:32:56,931 --> 00:32:58,019
Montana.

708
00:32:58,106 --> 00:33:00,848
No. Prison.

709
00:33:00,935 --> 00:33:03,285
Put your game face on,
cockroach

710
00:33:03,372 --> 00:33:05,026
for the dignity
of your family.

711
00:33:05,113 --> 00:33:08,638
Hello there.
I got something for you.

712
00:33:08,725 --> 00:33:09,944
I do.
Look at this.

713
00:33:10,031 --> 00:33:12,120
Marcy-grace!

714
00:33:12,207 --> 00:33:14,209
- Yes.
- Oh, that is so nice of you.

715
00:33:14,296 --> 00:33:16,211
- What do you say, honey?
- Thank you.

716
00:33:16,298 --> 00:33:18,170
It's my pleasure.

717
00:33:18,257 --> 00:33:20,346
I found something else
in your house.

718
00:33:20,433 --> 00:33:22,174
We have to get on the road

719
00:33:22,261 --> 00:33:24,306
so whatever it is,
just keep it, okay?

720
00:33:24,393 --> 00:33:26,656
Come on. Get in the truck.
Let's go.

721
00:33:26,743 --> 00:33:28,180
Wait, what's this about?

722
00:33:28,267 --> 00:33:30,008
Well, I don't believe
it's anything

723
00:33:30,095 --> 00:33:31,487
your daughter
wants to hear about.

724
00:33:32,793 --> 00:33:33,750
Honey?

725
00:33:35,535 --> 00:33:37,493
[sighs]
Take her into the room.

726
00:33:37,580 --> 00:33:39,930
Just go. I'll explain later.
It's okay.

727
00:33:41,976 --> 00:33:43,543
Come on, baby.

728
00:33:44,979 --> 00:33:46,981
Get crazy with
those cheese puffs, kid!

729
00:33:47,068 --> 00:33:48,722
So cute.

730
00:33:48,809 --> 00:33:50,506
Before I show you
what I found

731
00:33:50,593 --> 00:33:53,292
I have to tell you
the story that goes with it.

732
00:33:53,379 --> 00:33:56,686
Um, it's a tale of
an ATM robbery gone wrong.

733
00:33:56,773 --> 00:33:59,124
I believe you read about it
in the newspapers.

734
00:34:05,347 --> 00:34:06,348
Put the bags down!

735
00:34:06,435 --> 00:34:07,654
'Do it!'

736
00:34:07,741 --> 00:34:09,351
Now your guns. Slowly.

737
00:34:12,528 --> 00:34:14,748
[Patrick]
'Well, I don't believe
the masked robber'

738
00:34:14,835 --> 00:34:17,272
'intended to hurt anyone,
but then again, they rarely do.'

739
00:34:20,449 --> 00:34:22,147
'The first thing
a smart criminal does'

740
00:34:22,234 --> 00:34:23,757
'is destroy the evidence.'

741
00:34:23,844 --> 00:34:26,977
'Fire, that's a very
popular way to do that.'

742
00:34:27,065 --> 00:34:30,068
'With the fireplace alight,
the masked robber'

743
00:34:30,155 --> 00:34:33,593
'ran upstairs to lock his stash
away in the bedroom safe.'

744
00:34:33,680 --> 00:34:36,552
'What he didn't know
was the fire downstairs'

745
00:34:36,639 --> 00:34:40,556
'was now burning
out of control.'

746
00:34:40,643 --> 00:34:43,472
[coughing]

747
00:34:43,559 --> 00:34:46,258
Excuse me, but what
does this have to do with me?

748
00:34:46,345 --> 00:34:48,695
Bear with me.
It's been a rough week.

749
00:34:48,782 --> 00:34:50,044
Now when the masked robber

750
00:34:50,131 --> 00:34:52,307
found the bottom floor
of his house on fire

751
00:34:52,394 --> 00:34:53,569
he should have
ran out.

752
00:34:53,656 --> 00:34:55,745
[coughing]

753
00:34:55,832 --> 00:34:58,748
'He couldn't abandon the cash,
not after all that.'

754
00:34:58,835 --> 00:35:00,054
'So he ran back upstairs.'

755
00:35:00,141 --> 00:35:01,969
'Problem is the smoke
was too thick.'

756
00:35:02,056 --> 00:35:04,102
'He couldn't breathe.'

757
00:35:04,189 --> 00:35:05,407
'The masked robber
passed out'

758
00:35:05,494 --> 00:35:09,019
'right there
in his own bedroom.'

759
00:35:09,107 --> 00:35:10,891
Are you accusing me
of armed robbery?

760
00:35:10,978 --> 00:35:12,284
Yes.

761
00:35:12,371 --> 00:35:13,937
And murder.

762
00:35:14,024 --> 00:35:16,679
Oh, and, uh,
attempted murder, too.

763
00:35:16,766 --> 00:35:18,290
Mine.

764
00:35:18,377 --> 00:35:19,639
Well, you must
be desperate.

765
00:35:19,726 --> 00:35:22,816
Hardly. I had you
pegged from the start.

766
00:35:22,903 --> 00:35:24,644
You know how
armored guards operate

767
00:35:24,731 --> 00:35:26,211
from your
savings and loan days.

768
00:35:26,298 --> 00:35:29,866
And who would light a fire
on such a warm morning?

769
00:35:29,953 --> 00:35:32,304
The hard part
was to figure out

770
00:35:32,391 --> 00:35:35,307
what would drive you to kill
the man who saved your life?

771
00:35:37,309 --> 00:35:39,528
'Then I realized that
firefighter saw something'

772
00:35:39,615 --> 00:35:41,530
'when he braved those
flames to rescue you.'

773
00:35:41,617 --> 00:35:43,576
I've got an unconscious
man in here.

774
00:35:43,663 --> 00:35:46,100
[Patrick]
'Something that would
send you to jail for life'

775
00:35:46,187 --> 00:35:48,407
'when he caught the news
reports of the robbery'

776
00:35:48,494 --> 00:35:51,801
'and pieced together what he saw
inside that burning house.'

777
00:35:57,851 --> 00:35:59,983
The mask. It had to be.

778
00:36:00,070 --> 00:36:02,769
It's what you were trying
to burn with those bank bags.

779
00:36:02,856 --> 00:36:04,771
It's what Satterfield saw
in your bedroom

780
00:36:04,858 --> 00:36:06,773
and that's why
you had to kill him

781
00:36:08,905 --> 00:36:09,863
[knife slices]

782
00:36:11,952 --> 00:36:13,736
[grunting]

783
00:36:16,391 --> 00:36:17,914
That's a scary story.

784
00:36:18,001 --> 00:36:20,482
But without proof,
it's just a story.

785
00:36:20,569 --> 00:36:22,441
Oh, almost forgot

786
00:36:22,528 --> 00:36:25,139
the other thing
I found in the ashes.

787
00:36:26,706 --> 00:36:28,098
Yeah.

788
00:36:28,186 --> 00:36:30,231
I searched your house
before you did, remember?

789
00:36:30,318 --> 00:36:31,972
I actually tried
to crack your safe.

790
00:36:32,059 --> 00:36:34,453
It's not my specialty.

791
00:36:34,540 --> 00:36:36,542
Don't worry.
I'm not here to bust you.

792
00:36:36,629 --> 00:36:41,286
There's nothing in that for me.
I want half the money.

793
00:36:41,373 --> 00:36:42,461
Half the money.

794
00:36:42,548 --> 00:36:45,377
Well, I think
that's very generous.

795
00:36:45,464 --> 00:36:47,857
I could ask for more.
What choice do youhave?

796
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:01,828
[clears throat]

797
00:37:01,915 --> 00:37:03,612
[unzips bag]

798
00:37:14,057 --> 00:37:15,102
Half.

799
00:37:20,934 --> 00:37:22,370
Now the mask.

800
00:37:22,457 --> 00:37:24,764
Uh, yeah. The mask.
Of course, yes.

801
00:37:24,851 --> 00:37:26,548
You can have that there.

802
00:37:37,385 --> 00:37:39,605
Wait. This isn't mine.

803
00:37:39,692 --> 00:37:41,955
No. I found that
at a Mexican party store.

804
00:37:42,042 --> 00:37:44,610
The colors are a good match,
though, don't you think?

805
00:37:44,697 --> 00:37:46,133
Hands in the air.

806
00:37:46,220 --> 00:37:47,265
Do it now!

807
00:37:47,352 --> 00:37:49,615
[siren wailing]

808
00:37:54,272 --> 00:37:55,534
Turn around.

809
00:38:05,065 --> 00:38:06,284
[indistinct chattering]

810
00:38:06,371 --> 00:38:08,460
I've always wanted
a couch like that.

811
00:38:08,547 --> 00:38:10,418
Who's this?

812
00:38:10,505 --> 00:38:13,291
Oh, this is my responsible
adult, Tamara.

813
00:38:13,378 --> 00:38:14,335
Ta-mara.

814
00:38:14,422 --> 00:38:17,338
Hi.
Hi.

815
00:38:17,425 --> 00:38:19,079
Well, you did it.

816
00:38:19,166 --> 00:38:22,517
You caught a killer, and we got
back most of the stolen cash.

817
00:38:22,604 --> 00:38:24,345
Most?

818
00:38:24,432 --> 00:38:26,129
Wilcox was missing
some of the money.

819
00:38:26,216 --> 00:38:28,915
He said you took it,
but we didn't believe it.

820
00:38:29,002 --> 00:38:30,873
Hmm.
I'd look for an accomplice.

821
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:33,789
That's a big job
to pull off alone.

822
00:38:33,876 --> 00:38:35,008
We'll do that.

823
00:38:35,095 --> 00:38:37,619
Oh, by the way.

824
00:38:37,706 --> 00:38:38,620
Your last paycheck.

825
00:38:42,972 --> 00:38:44,887
That's my payment?

826
00:38:44,974 --> 00:38:47,325
You weren't exactly
in it for the money.

827
00:38:47,412 --> 00:38:48,848
I'll confess
to a vague satisfaction

828
00:38:48,935 --> 00:38:50,240
in taking down someone

829
00:38:50,328 --> 00:38:52,025
that thought they were
smarter than me.

830
00:38:52,112 --> 00:38:54,941
But not enough
to wanna stick around here.

831
00:38:55,028 --> 00:38:57,683
Alright. Well, let me know
if you change your mind

832
00:38:57,770 --> 00:38:59,249
or, you know, get it back.

833
00:38:59,337 --> 00:39:00,860
Some doors
are best left shut.

834
00:39:00,947 --> 00:39:04,167
Uh, everyone else, I'd just
like to say thank you.

835
00:39:04,254 --> 00:39:07,780
I'm sure we've shared
some great times together.

836
00:39:07,867 --> 00:39:09,651
I'm not presently
qualified to comment,

837
00:39:09,738 --> 00:39:12,437
so I'm just gonna make
like a rock and roll.

838
00:39:12,524 --> 00:39:13,525
Bye.

839
00:39:16,441 --> 00:39:19,052
Wait.

840
00:39:19,139 --> 00:39:20,183
That looks real.

841
00:39:20,270 --> 00:39:21,315
Sure does.

842
00:39:21,402 --> 00:39:22,708
[chuckling]

843
00:39:22,795 --> 00:39:23,839
Mmm.

844
00:39:23,926 --> 00:39:25,841
Youweren't lying.

845
00:39:25,928 --> 00:39:27,190
Huh.

846
00:39:27,277 --> 00:39:29,236
You took it.

847
00:39:29,323 --> 00:39:34,546
Wilcox wasn't lying.
You really took it.

848
00:39:34,633 --> 00:39:37,113
I'm sure I have no idea
what you're talking about.

849
00:39:37,200 --> 00:39:40,900
I'm late for my new life.

850
00:39:40,987 --> 00:39:42,380
When did you grab
the cash?

851
00:39:43,859 --> 00:39:45,339
Oh, please,
does it really matter?

852
00:39:45,426 --> 00:39:47,341
I mean, you people are
the highway robbers.

853
00:39:47,428 --> 00:39:48,386
Look at this paycheck!

854
00:39:48,473 --> 00:39:50,344
We could have you arrested.

855
00:39:50,431 --> 00:39:53,913
No one can convict me.
I'm out of my mind.

856
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:55,131
Ask my doctor.

857
00:39:55,218 --> 00:39:56,655
You're running away.

858
00:39:56,742 --> 00:39:58,352
What are you talking about?

859
00:39:58,439 --> 00:39:59,875
You're starting to feel
something inside

860
00:39:59,962 --> 00:40:01,921
and you don't know
what to do.

861
00:40:02,008 --> 00:40:03,139
That's nonsense.

862
00:40:03,226 --> 00:40:05,228
Prove it.
Take a ride with me.

863
00:40:05,315 --> 00:40:07,579
If you still wanna leave
after that, you can.

864
00:40:26,902 --> 00:40:28,121
[car door closes]

865
00:40:29,644 --> 00:40:32,560
Great house.
Whose is it?

866
00:40:32,647 --> 00:40:34,562
It's yours.

867
00:40:34,649 --> 00:40:36,782
Give me your keys.

868
00:40:36,869 --> 00:40:38,348
Okay.

869
00:40:38,436 --> 00:40:41,264
[keys jangle, lock turning]

870
00:40:41,351 --> 00:40:42,309
[chuckling]

871
00:40:46,705 --> 00:40:48,620
[slow instrumental music]

872
00:41:15,037 --> 00:41:16,604
[music continues]

873
00:41:34,796 --> 00:41:36,624
[music continues]

874
00:42:00,605 --> 00:42:01,736
I'm sorry.

